  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Q10 - Multipage data - households and individuals</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Two related data frames: what are we averaging?</h3><p>A survey records four households and the people living in them. The first data frame contains one row per household.</p><table class="table table-bordered"><thead><tr><th>Household ID</th><th>Number of members</th><th>Monthly household income (units)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>H1</td><td>{@n1@}</td><td>{@inc1@}</td></tr><tr><td>H2</td><td>{@n2@}</td><td>{@inc2@}</td></tr><tr><td>H3</td><td>{@n3@}</td><td>{@inc3@}</td></tr><tr><td>H4</td><td>{@n4@}</td><td>{@inc4@}</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The second data frame has one row for each individual. It contains that person's household ID and repeats their household's income on every member's row.</p><h4>A. Identify the observational units</h4><p>What does one row of the household data frame represent? [[input:unit1]] [[validation:unit1]]</p><p>What does one row of the individual data frame represent? [[input:unit2]] [[validation:unit2]]</p><h4>B. Predict before calculating</h4><p>If we calculate the mean of the household-income column in each data frame, which result do you predict? [[input:predict]] [[validation:predict]]</p><h4>C. Investigate the data</h4><p>How many households are represented? [[input:houses]] [[validation:houses]]</p><p>How many individuals are represented? [[input:people]] [[validation:people]]</p><p>Calculate the mean household income using the household data frame. [[input:meanhouse]] [[validation:meanhouse]]</p><p>Calculate the mean of the repeated household-income column using the individual data frame. Give your answer to two decimal places if needed. [[input:meanperson]] [[validation:meanperson]]</p>
[[hint title="Hint: repeated incomes"]]<p>In the individual data frame, multiply each household income by its number of members, add these products and divide by the total number of individuals.</p>[[/hint]]
<h4>D. Interpret</h4><p>Why do the two means differ, and which calculation answers the question, "What is the mean income across households?" [[input:interpret]] [[validation:interpret]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked solution</h3><p>In the first data frame each row is a household; in the second each row is an individual. There are {@nh@} households and {@ni@} individuals.</p><p>Because larger households also have higher incomes in this example, repeating each household income once per person makes the individual-level mean higher than the household-level mean.</p><p><strong>Equal weight for each household:</strong></p>
\[
\frac{{@inc1@}+{@inc2@}+{@inc3@}+{@inc4@}}{4}={@mean_h@}.
\]
<p><strong>One copy of household income for each individual:</strong></p>
\[
\frac{{@n1@}\cdot {@inc1@}+{@n2@}\cdot {@inc2@}+{@n3@}\cdot {@inc3@}+{@n4@}\cdot {@inc4@}}{@ni@}={@mean_i@}\approx {@float(mean_i)@}.
\]
<p>Both are valid calculations, but they answer different questions. To find the mean income across households, give each household equal weight and use the first mean. The second is the mean household income associated with a randomly selected individual in these data.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
